{
  "dom_id": "step-q85254",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "The manager at a water bottle factory notices that, on average, every batch of water bottles has $7$ water bottles on which the label is upside down. What is the probability that the next batch of water bottles will have more than $2$ bottles on which the label is upside down? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that upside-down labels occur independently and at a constant average rate.",
  "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of water bottles on which the label is upside down. Since the average rate is $7$ upside-down labels per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of upside-down labels can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>2),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=1-P(X\\le 2)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}{e}^{-7}}{0!}+\\frac{{7}^{1}{e}^{-7}}{1!}+\\frac{{7}^{2}{e}^{-7}}{2!} \\\\  & ≈0.030\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding more than $2$ upside-down labels in the next batch is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.030 \\\\  & =0.970\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
